   "title": "Two ways to let a model write the policy",
   "desc": "Graph-as-Policy is the target, but it runs as a separate process and wants an RTX 4090, an API key and 3.5 GB of weights. This pod has no GPU at all, so no graph has produced an episode here. Code as Policies has the same shape — an instruction goes in, a policy comes out — but what it emits is text, so it runs anywhere. A generator writes Python against a small API, a sandbox executes it under bounded names, calls and wall clock, and the workcell’s own primitives do every motion. Only the choice of sequence changes between that and the scripted expert, which is what makes the two comparable at all. Each episode stores the exact program that drove it and checks it against its digest before running. The measured result is negative: containment fell from 1 of 3 to 0 of 3. That was the workcell’s 82 commits rather than the policy, and it took a control run on each revision to tell them apart.",
